; APPLY of an interpreted &rest function to N arguments (lisp/apply-large-frame.lisp).
; UAPLY spreads the list onto the stack, and the frame then holds N + 4 words
; when INTP1 opens APPLY-LAMBDA's call block, whose deltas have 8 bits: at
; N = 252 (256 words) they wrap and microcode 2002 used to halt the machine at
; QMXSG's ILLOP, and the case got no answer.  The spread now stops at %SPREAD's
; limit, 370 octal words of frame, and signals its error,
; STACK-FRAME-TOO-LARGE past 249 arguments.  The call block INTP1 opens is
; refused at the same limit, the frame with it 370 octal words
; (call-block-large-frame): up to 245 arguments the call returns N; past them
; it signals the error, and the listener answers afterwards.
(compiled-function-p #'alf-plain) => NIL
(alf-plain-n 10) => 10
(alf-plain-n 245) => 245
(alf-n 245) => 245
(alf-plain-n 246) =>! stack frame larger than 256
(alf-n 246) =>! stack frame larger than 256
(alf-plain-n 249) =>! stack frame larger than 256
(alf-plain-n 250) =>! stack frame larger than 256
(alf-plain-n 252) =>! stack frame larger than 256
(alf-plain-n 300) =>! stack frame larger than 256
(alf-plain-n 600) =>! stack frame larger than 256
(alf-n 300) =>! stack frame larger than 256
(alf-n 600) =>! stack frame larger than 256
(alf-plain-n 10) => 10
(alf-n 10) => 10
